<header><p class="enc-kicker">Historical factor</p><h1 class="enc-title">State monopolies</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">State monopolies refers to the recurring pattern in which governments reserved control of valuable goods or trades for themselves or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartered-company">chartered companies</a>. Monopolies generated revenue but could encourage smuggling and inefficiency. Examples include the salt and iron monopoly of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Empire</a>'s controlled economy, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin Empire</a>'s royal trade.</p></div>
